<onlyinclude>'''Semiconductor fabrication''' makes microscopic electronic components by repeatedly adding, changing, and removing materials on a carefully prepared wafer. A cleanroom factory (''fab'') carries out these operations under strict contamination and process controls. The result is a wafer containing many individual electronic dies, not yet finished packaged chips.</onlyinclude>

== How a wafer becomes circuitry ==
A polished wafer is cleaned and may receive an insulating oxide. Thin films are grown or deposited; light-sensitive resist and lithography define patterns; etching removes selected exposed material; implantation or diffusion introduces dopants; and heat treatment adjusts material properties. Chemical-mechanical planarization smooths surfaces for further layers. Later metallization and dielectric layers form connections between devices. The steps recur in different orders according to the device and process design.<ref name="sia">[https://www.semiconductors.org/semiconductors-101/how-are-semiconductors-made/stage-3-front-end-manufacturing/ Semiconductor Industry Association, ''Stage 3: Front-end Manufacturing''].</ref><ref name="asml">[https://www.asml.com/en/technology/all-about-microchips/how-microchips-are-made ASML, ''How microchips are made''].</ref>

== Why control matters ==
A small particle, inaccurate alignment, unsuitable film thickness, or incorrect dopant profile can make a device fail. Engineers measure dimensions and defects throughout production, adjust equipment, and electrically test completed wafers. ''Yield'' is the fraction of potential devices that meet specified criteria; it depends on design, defects, process variation, and testing definitions.

== Boundaries ==
A ''technology node'' is a process-family designation, not a single universally measured transistor dimension. Fabrication is the manufacturing workflow; lithography is one patterning step, while deposition and etching are separate unit processes. Wafer probing, dicing, packaging, and final testing may follow wafer fabrication.<ref name="nist">[https://www.nist.gov/system/files/documents/2024/06/28/Final%20PEA%20for%20Modernization%20and%20Expansion%20of%20Semiconductor%20Fabs%206-28-2024%20-%20OGC-508C.pdf NIST, ''Final Programmatic Environmental Assessment: Modernization and Expansion of Existing Semiconductor Fabrication Facilities'' (2024)].</ref>
